    "title": "FSU PC to host free youth football camp with Javien Elliot and Boris Jackson - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Stephanie Pitts No media credit provided. ,\n12 hours ago\nPANAMA CITY, Fla. ( WMBB ) – Florida State University Panama City is hosting a free youth football camp led by a former NFL player and the founder of Speed Plus Sports.\nThe Panama City native, Javien Elliot, a former defensive back for the Florida State Seminoles, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Carolina Panthers, and the Canadian Football League, will be on the field for the one-day event.\nCoach Boris Jackson , with several years of coaching experience and the founder of the training program, Speed Plus Sports, will help participants learn game fundamentals and improve their skills.\nThe free camp will take place on June 6 at Tommy Oliver Stadium in Panama City. It is open to ages nine and up and all genders.\nRegistration starts at 8 a.m. and skills sessions begin at 9 a.m. The camp will run until 12 p.m. with pizza and refreshments provided.\nParticipants will be split into groups depending on their ages and skills. The sessions will be in flag football style with no contact.\nGuardians must submit a separate registration form and waiver for each child participating in the camp. The form can be sent to outreach@pc.fsu.edu or brought on the day of the event. The form can be found here .\nFor more information, click here .\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to mypanhandle.com.",
    "summary": ", PANAMA CITY, Fla. ( WMBB ) – Florida State University Panama City is hosting a free youth football camp led by a former NFL player and the founder of Speed Plus Sports. The Panama City native, Javien Elliot, a former defensive back for the Florida State Seminoles, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Carolina Panthers, and the Canadian Football League, will be on the field for the one-day event. The free camp will take place on June 6 at Tommy Oliver Stadium in Panama City. Guardians must submit a separate registration form and waiver for each child participating in the camp.",
